%description -n texlive-cyber
This LaTeX package helps you write documents indicating your compliance with
cybersecurity requirements. It also helps you format your document in a form
suitable inside the U.S. Department of Defense, by attaching distribution
statements, destruction notices, organization logos, and security labels to it.

%package -n texlive-cybercic
Summary:        "Controls in Contents" for the cyber package
Version:        svn37659
License:        MIT
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-cybercic-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-cybercic-doc <= 11:%{version}

%description -n texlive-cybercic
This package is used in concert with the cyber package to make documents with
annotations of compliance with cybersecurity requirements. "cic" stands for
"Controls in Contents", and when you include this package, some notations of
compliance are added to section names as seen in the table of contents of the
final document. It also makes your document more brittle in unexpected ways:
for example, when you use cybercic in the same document as hyperref, you cannot
use any formatting in your section titles. So don't use cybercic unless you
need to.

%package -n texlive-darkmode
Summary:        General Dark Mode Support for LaTeX-Documents
Version:        svn76924
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
