The bundle allows the user to create Unified Process methodology (UP or RUP)
based documents. The style provides document versioning, document history,
document authors, document validators, specification description, task
management, and several helping macros.

%package -n texlive-upquote
Summary:        Show "realistic" quotes in verbatim
Version:        svn77682
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-upquote-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-upquote-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(textcomp.sty)

%description -n texlive-upquote
Typewriter-style fonts are best for program listings, but Computer Modern
Typewriter prints ` and ' as bent opening and closing single quotes. Other
fonts, and most programming languages, print ` as a grave accent and ' upright;
' is used both to open and to close quoted strings. The package switches the
typewriter font to Computer Modern Typewriter in OT1 encoding, and modifies the
behaviour of verbatim, verbatim*, \verb, and \verb* to print in the "` and '
way". It does this regardless of other fonts or encodings in use, so long as
the package is loaded after the other fonts were. The package does not affect
\tt, \texttt, etc.

%package -n texlive-uri
Summary:        Hyperlinks for a wide range of URIs
Version:        svn77682
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-uri-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-uri-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(kvoptions.sty)
Requires:       tex(url.sty)

%description -n texlive-uri
The package provides automatic hyperlinks for URIs of type arXiv, ASIN, DOI,
HDL, NBN, OCLC, OID, PubMed, TINY, TINY with preview, and WebCite and provides
commands \citeurl, \mailto, \ukoeln, and \uref.

%package -n texlive-useclass
Summary:        Load classes as packages
Version:        svn76924
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea

%description -n texlive-useclass
This package enables the usage of classes as packages. It was originally
designed for the l3doc class, which is employed for documenting LaTeX code with
numerous useful features. However, employing the l3doc class as a package is
not convenient when using the developing class as the style for the document.
Therefore, this package provides a simple interface for utilizing classes as
packages.

%package -n texlive-user-persona
Summary:        Typesetting User Persona sheets for UX and HCI (Human-Computer Interaction)
Version:        svn78798
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Requires:       tex(enumitem.sty)
Requires:       tex(etoolbox.sty)
Requires:       tex(graphicx.sty)
Requires:       tex(tabularx.sty)
Requires:       tex(tcolorbox.sty)
Requires:       tex(xcolor.sty)

%description -n texlive-user-persona
This package provides a structured environment and command set for typesetting
professional user persona sheets in LaTeX. A user persona is a
research-grounded representation of a target user group, synthesizing observed
behaviours, goals, and frustrations into a humanized archetype (Cooper, 1999).
The package is intended for User Experience (UX) researchers, Human-Computer
Interaction (HCI) practitioners, and product designers who maintain
documentation within a LaTeX workflow. The central persona environment produces
a two-column layout comprising a portrait image, an identity table, a
pull-quote, and a set of colour-coded content cards. Cards may be placed in the
left column, right column, or as a full-width span below both columns.
Predefined semantic colours are supplied for common persona sections (Goals,
Behaviours, Pain Points, Context). Inline pill labels and icon badges are
supported via \personapill and \personabrand (using fontawesome5).

%package -n texlive-ushort
Summary:        Shorter (and longer) underlines and underbars
Version:        svn32261
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-ushort-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-ushort-doc <= 11:%{version}

%description -n texlive-ushort
Some engineers need underlined or twice underlined variables for which the
usual \underline is too long. This package provides a generic command for
creating underlines of various sizes and types.

%package -n texlive-uspace
Summary:        Giving meaning to various Unicode space characters
Version:        svn63123
License:        MIT
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Requires:       tex(ifluatex.sty)
Requires:       tex(ifxetex.sty)
Requires:       tex(inputenc.sty)
Requires:       tex(newunicodechar.sty)

%description -n texlive-uspace
LaTeX package that gives meaning to various Unicode space characters.

%package -n texlive-utf8add
Summary:        Additional support for UTF-8 encoded LaTeX input
Version:        svn61074
License:        GPL-3.0-or-later
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Requires:       tex(amsmath.sty)
Requires:       tex(gensymb.sty)
Requires:       tex(inputenc.sty)
Requires:       tex(nicefrac.sty)
Requires:       tex(upgreek.sty)
Requires:       tex(xkeyval.sty)

%description -n texlive-utf8add
This bundle contains the LaTeX packages utf8add.sty and utf8hax.sty. The
utf8add package provides additional support for the use of UTF-8 encoded input.
This is intended for making LaTeX input more readable. The utf8hax package is
using UTF-8 characters for easier access to math in LaTeX, however making the
LaTeX input less readable.

%package -n texlive-uwmslide
Summary:        Slides with a simple Power Point like appearance
Version:        svn27354
License:        Artistic-2.0
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea

%description -n texlive-uwmslide
A slide format which produces slides with a simple Power Point like appearance.
Several useful features include: use of standard titlepage to produce title
slide; several slide environments including plain (page with a title), double
slide (two column page with slide title), item slide (item list with title),
left item slide, and right item slide. Logos are placed in the upper left
corner of each slide if the logo file logo.eps is present. Preconfigured in
landscape mode by default and uses Times Roman by default (originally, it was
claimed, for simple conversion to PDF format).

%package -n texlive-variablelm
Summary:        Font definitions for the variable Latin Modern fonts
Version:        svn60014
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Requires:       tex(amsmath.sty)
Requires:       tex(fontenc.sty)
Requires:       tex(xkeyval.sty)

%description -n texlive-variablelm
This package provides a mechanism for scaling a typeface. It is directed at the
Latin Modern fonts and provides the font definitions and the corresponding
style file. This mechanism is useful in mixed text compositions, for example
Japanese-Latin.

%package -n texlive-varindex
Summary:        Luxury frontend to the \index command
Version:        svn32262
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-varindex-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-varindex-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(toolbox.sty)

%description -n texlive-varindex
Provides a convenient front-end for the \index command. For example, with it
you can generate multiple index entries in almost any form by a single command.
The package is highly customizable, and works with all versions of LaTeX and
probably most other TeX formats.

%package -n texlive-varsfromjobname
Summary:        Extract variables from the name of the LaTeX file
Version:        svn76924
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-varsfromjobname-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-varsfromjobname-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(currfile.sty)

%description -n texlive-varsfromjobname
The package allows the user to extract information from the job name, provided
that the name has been structured appropriately: the package expects the file
name to consist of a set of words separated by hyphens.

%package -n texlive-varwidth
Summary:        A variable-width minipage
Version:        svn77682
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-varwidth-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-varwidth-doc <= 11:%{version}

%description -n texlive-varwidth
The varwidth environment is superficially similar to minipage, but the
specified width is just a maximum value -- the box may get a narrower "natural"
width.

%package -n texlive-vcell
Summary:        Vertical alignment of content inside table cells
Version:        svn77682
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea

%description -n texlive-vcell
This package offers low-level macros to build rows with vertically-aligned
cells (top, middle or bottom) and calculate the height of a row. These cells
can have variable or fixed height and can be paragraph-cells or inline-cells.
Different vertical alignments can be used in the same row.

%package -n texlive-vdmlisting
Summary:        Typesetting VDM in ASCII syntax
Version:        svn56905
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-vdmlisting-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-vdmlisting-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(color.sty)
Requires:       tex(listings.sty)
Requires:       tex(times.sty)

%description -n texlive-vdmlisting
The package is an extension for the listings package that provides a source
code printer for LaTeX. This package defines new language definitions and
listing environments for the three language dialects of the Vienna Development
Method: VDM-SL, VDM-PP and VDM-RT. If one wants to typeset VDM with a
mathematical syntax instead of the ASCII syntax used here one should use the
vdm pacakge instead

%package -n texlive-verbasef
Summary:        VERBatim Automatic Splitting of External Files
Version:        svn21922
License:        GPL-2.0-or-later
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-verbasef-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-verbasef-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(here.sty)
Requires:       tex(verbatim.sty)
Requires:       tex(vrbexin.sty)

%description -n texlive-verbasef
The package allows you to input (subsections of a) file, print them in verbatim
mode, while automatically breaking up the input lines into pieces of a given
length, which are output as figures. These figures are posted using the [H]
specification, which forces LaTeX to place the figure at the spot of
invocation, rather than floating the figures to the top of the next page. The
package requires the verbatim, here and vrbexin packages.

%package -n texlive-verbatimbox
Summary:        Deposit verbatim text in a box
Version:        svn77682
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-verbatimbox-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-verbatimbox-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(readarray.sty)
Requires:       tex(verbatim.sty)

%description -n texlive-verbatimbox
The package provides a verbbox environment (which uses techniques similar to
those of the boxedverbatim environment of the moreverb package) to place its
contents into a globally available box, or into a box specified by the user.
The global box may then be used in a variety of situations (for example,
providing a replica of the boxedverbatim environment itself). A valuable use is
in places where the standard verbatim environment (which is based on a
trivlist) may not appear. The package makes use of the verbatim package (which
is a required part of any LaTeX distribution).

%package -n texlive-verbatimcopy
Summary:        Make copies of text documents from within LaTeX
Version:        svn15878
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-verbatimcopy-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-verbatimcopy-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(verbatim.sty)

%description -n texlive-verbatimcopy
This package provides \VerbatimCopy{in}{out} that will enable LaTeX to take a
verbatim copy of one text file, and save it under another name. The package
provides a means to specify the output directory to be used, but does no
checking and may therefore overwrite an important file if used injudiciously.

%package -n texlive-verbdef
Summary:        Define commands which expand to verbatim text
Version:        svn77682
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-verbdef-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-verbdef-doc <= 11:%{version}

%description -n texlive-verbdef
The package defines a single command \verbdef (which has a *-form, like \verb).
\verbdef will define a robust command whose body expands to verbatim text. By
using commands defined by \verbdef, one can put verbatim text into the
arguments of commands; since the defined command is robust, it doesn't matter
if the argument is moving. (Full details of syntax and caveats about use are in
comments in the file itself.)

%package -n texlive-verbments
Summary:        Syntax highlighting of source code in LaTeX documents
Version:        svn23670
License:        LPPL-1.3c
Requires:       texlive-base
Requires:       texlive-kpathsea
Provides:       texlive-verbments-doc = %{epoch}:%{version}-%{release}
Obsoletes:      texlive-verbments-doc <= 11:%{version}
Requires:       tex(calc.sty)
